questions.stem:
A pipe reader runs this pseudocode, where buf_lock is the condition lock that protects the emptiness check and data is an integer count of items available:

reader() {
  while (1) {
    acquire(&buf_lock);
    if (data == 0)
      continue_wait();
    consume_one();
    release(&buf_lock);
  }
}

continue_wait() {
  release(&buf_lock);
  sleep(buf_chan);
  acquire(&buf_lock);
}

A pipe writer runs this pseudocode:

writer() {
  acquire(&buf_lock);
  data++;
  release(&buf_lock);
  wakeup(buf_chan);
}

The interface is xv6's sleep_prepare(chan), sleep(chan), and wakeup(chan). sleep_prepare(chan) records chan in p->chan and returns. sleep(chan) returns immediately if p->chan is zero; otherwise it sets the process SLEEPING and calls sched(). wakeup(chan) wakes all processes sleeping on chan, and also sets p->chan to zero for any process that has called sleep_prepare(chan) but has not yet slept on chan. All processes and locks are otherwise correct except for the one flaw introduced by the modification below.

Suppose a student replaces
  continue_wait();
with this single modified line inside the reader's if (data == 0) branch, all other code unchanged:

  release(&buf_lock); sleep_prepare(buf_chan); sleep(buf_chan); acquire(&buf_lock);

Consider the following events; all values start at data = 0 and the reader begins first:
1. Reader acquires buf_lock, executes if (data == 0), and enters its modified branch.
2. Reader releases buf_lock.
3. Writer executes writer() completely: it acquires buf_lock, increments data to 1, releases buf_lock, and calls wakeup(buf_chan).
4. Reader then calls sleep_prepare(buf_chan) and sleep(buf_chan).

Which statement correctly explains the outcome, and why, under ordinary assumptions that step 3 completes before step 4 begins?
